 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Partly, but Perth isn't the main reason.** Electricity prices have risen sharply across Australia, while Perth's year-on-year change has been small recently.\n\n- **Nationally**, electricity prices in August 2026 were **13.2% higher** than in August 2025 (index 113.7 vs 100.5).\n- **Perth** was **2.4% higher** in August 2026 than in August 2025 (index 102.4 vs 100.0). Perth's price level has been roughly flat since around July 2025.\n- **The Perth figures are distorted by government rebates.** Perth's electricity prices were heavily discounted through 2024 and early 2025, and when those rebates ended, the year-on-year changes swung by hundreds of percent (for example, +289% in August 2025). So the price was already much higher than in 2024 and early 2025, and that step-up is what your bill may reflect if it's been high for a while.\n\nThe CPI shows average prices, not your bill, so it can't tell you whether your bill is unusual. Things that commonly make a bill feel big:\n\n- **Usage**: heating or cooling, an electric hot water system, pool pumps, or an older fridge or freezer.\n- **Billing period**: some bills cover more days than others.\n- **Rebate status**: check whether a state or federal rebate was applied or has ended.\n- **Tariff**: check whether you're on a plan with a high daily supply charge, and compare plans on the Energy Made Easy comparison site.\n- **Meter read**: an estimated read can make a bill look too high. Compare the meter reading on the bill with your own reading.\n\nIf you share your last two or three bills (usage in kWh, daily supply charge, and total), I can help you work out whether the increase is from price or usage.",
